Date: Fri,  7 Jan 94 10:09:37 PST
From: Blanc Weber <blancw@microsoft.com>
Subject: FUND: game rules (for The Mothers of Invention)

>From Stuart Cards:

I think we need to figure out how to change the game rules.
Because it appears that as long as the current rules pertain,
things will not evolve in the direction we would like.

How can we change the nature of the game so that the choice
which directly and immediately benefits the individual
ALSO drives the system in the direction we want?
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Yes, this is important to think about.  I was reviewing an article 
yesterday which I had cut out of the New York Times Magazine some years 
ago, about an organization in Hungary which called itself the Free 
Democrats, and the difficulties they were having trying to accomplish 
their goals.  This section struck me as most relevant:

"When the old leaders saw the handwriting on the wall and began 
repackaging themselves as reform Communists, the true reformers saw 
nothing but the same cynicism, dishonesty and lack of principle that 
had characterized the Government for 40 years.  In an absurd reversal, 
one Government paper in Hungary recently attacked the Free Democrat 
leaders as "former vehement Marxist-Leninists."  A turnaround of such 
breathtaking swiftness could only mean one thing:  the reform 
Communists did not believe in democracy any more than they had in the 
Marxism they now attacked.  What they did believe in was power.

This cynicism the Free Democrats understood and hated.  To them, 
Communist rule had meant more than badly run state economies, prison 
for dissidents, and the absence of civil rights.  On the most personal 
level, it meant the corruption of individuals, the forced sacrifice of 
beliefs and principles to get along, to keep a job, obtain an apartment 
- to live.

This moral corruption is the principal target of the revolutions of 
1989 and 1990.  For 40 years, the people's energy has gone into 
finessing, compromising, trimming, finding the loophole in absurd 
bureaucratic dictums.

The revolutionaries take the position that the whole system must be 
swept away from top to bottom if people are ever to regain their dignity."

I wouldn't recommend trying this by yourself at home, but I can very 
well see the easy path to moral corruption.  I think that it is of 
essential importance, if there is no other way to live but in 
cooperation with "absurd bureaucratic dictums", that one nevertheless 
does not comply from non-valid reasoning; that one does not take 
someone else's argument as sufficient cause to "start one's engine", or 
explain one's own responses to a circumstance.

A person should see one's own mind as the source of one's motivation; 
should hold only oneself as accountable for the method one has seen fit 
to employ in dealing with a situation.

Blanc

