From: eder@hsvaic.hv.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Children as Property

To 'own property' means having the right to dispose of that
property in whatever way one wishes.  For example, as the
owner of a house, I have the right to destroy it if I so
desire (not that I'd want to), but no one else has the right
to do so.  I have the sole right to sell the house for something
in exchange, and so on.

It logically follows that in a fully free society, If I own
myself, I have the right to destroy myself (by suicide or
by doing stupid, dangerous things), and I have the right to
sell myself (into slavery, or to sell portions of my life
- employment for hire)

The interesting question is whether one automatically obtains
property ownership in one's self at birth.  I find this logically
inconsistent.  Any other product of one's labor is considered
ones property.   This includes products of one's body (breast milk
and long hair are currently saleable products, and a spare
kidney and blood could be sold also).  A child is just another
product of the body, which the mother has invested much effort
in creating, and which the parents then expend much further
effort in raising - i.e. converting from a useless raw material -
a baby - into a useful end product - an adult.

A baby at birth provided none of the effort to create itself -
all the raw materials came from the mother (minus one sperm from
the father), and the mother provided the support system - the
womb, etc.  Even if you consider the baby to have put in the
effort itself to take the raw materials and self-grow, I would
claim that the parents at least have an interest in the life-property
to the extent they contributed to it.

In this view, you own your own life, but your parents stick you
with a bill for child-rearing at the time you are old enough to
contract.  Then you can pay them back over time, or go to a lender,
borrow the money to pay off your parents, then pay the lender
back over time.

Note: I would expect that a lot of parents would gift their children
by writing off the debt to a large extent - but I am trying to
describe a logically consistent system.

Dani Eder

