Subject: BOT: How do trees drink?

Ray Cromwell says:

> Perhaps the tree uses "pumping stations" ...

  None mentioned in Botany 1.

> What you seem to be describing is capillary action.

  Not quite; the "pull-on-the-wire" mechanism involves evaporative losses at
the leaves, or wherever.  There has to be energy input somewhere -- for if
one could use capillary action to raise the water a ways, then let it fall
back to its original level, extracting energy as it goes, then back into the
capillary system for another trip, all with no extra energy added, one would
have a perpetual motion machine.

  The botanical references I checked on this matter were a little vague on
the details of the mechanism whereby tension was created at the top of the
water column, or perhaps I was not smart enough to understand them.  However,
I made an estimate of the inter-molecular forces between water molecules, and
found it more than enough to hold up a column 100 meters high.

